| Cognition and culture || Learned patterns, practices, symbols and records || Learning, communication, teaching and imitation || Cultural transmission differs from molecular reproduction
|}

== Biological inheritance ==
Molecular genetics supplies mechanisms for transmission of sequence information. Bacteria can also acquire genes horizontally through transformation, transduction or conjugation and related pathways. Reviews of transgenerational epigenetic inheritance describe replicative and reconstructive mechanisms; claims must be species- and generation-specific, and transgenerational effects in mammals are especially constrained.

Natural selection changes the frequency of heritable variants across generations; selection, transmission and mutation are analytically different processes. A genetic code is not a general proof that the whole cell is simply a digital computer.

== Software, agents and provenance ==
An agent may inherit stored tools, procedures, memories or task state from a predecessor. Valid continuity depends on authentication, versioning, provenance, permission scope and transformation rules. A copy should be represented as ''derived from'' its origin, not silently treated as the original identity. W3C PROV-O distinguishes entities, activities and agents in representing derivation and responsibility.

== Information-theoretic caution ==
[[Bit|Bits]] and [[Entropy]] can measure uncertainty or dependence in a modeled information channel; they do not by themselves define what is inherited or why it functions. Identical strings can have different roles, and inherited function depends on context, interpretation and environment. Shannon information, genetic inheritance, cultural transmission, and operational persistence must not be flattened into one ontology simply because all can involve information.

== Epistemic notes ==
The listed biological and computing mechanisms are established in their respective domains. The single label ''informational inheritance'' is a proposed bridge for comparison. A claim of universal substrate-independent inheritance law would require formal specification and evidence not supplied by the analogy.

